    <!-- ── By country ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
         Query family: "us ring size to uk", "uk ring size to us", "ring size
         m in numbers", "t ring size in mm", "european ring size", "eu ring
         size to us", "japanese ring size", "indian ring size chart", "italian
         ring size" (SEO-STRATEGY.md §9, R5). What each label means and a
         worked conversion both ways. The long "why charts disagree" argument
         lives on the homepage (#disagree) and is linked, never restated here —
         two copies of it would compete. -->
    <section class="mt-16" aria-labelledby="systems">
      <h2 id="systems" class="text-[clamp(1.75rem,3.5vw,2.25rem)]">How each country’s sizes work</h2>

      <p class="mt-5 max-w-[62ch] text-text-muted">
        Every system is a different label for the same inside measurement. Here is what each label
        means, how to convert it both ways, and where charts for it disagree.
      </p>

      <h3 id="us" class="mt-10 text-xl font-semibold">US and Canada</h3>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-[62ch] text-text-muted">
        No standards body defines US sizes; every maker uses the same trade rule. Size&nbsp;0 is
        0.458&nbsp;in ({us0Mm.toFixed(2)}&nbsp;mm) across the inside and each whole size adds
        0.032&nbsp;in ({US_STEP_MM.toFixed(4)}&nbsp;mm), so a quarter size — the smallest step sold —
        is {usQuarterMm.toFixed(4)}&nbsp;mm. Canada uses the same scale.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-[62ch] text-text-muted">
        A US&nbsp;7 is {us0Mm.toFixed(4)} + 7&nbsp;×&nbsp;{US_STEP_MM.toFixed(4)} ={' '}
        <strong class="text-text tabular">{us7.diameterMm.toFixed(2)}&nbsp;mm</strong> across and
        {' '}{us7.circumferenceMm.toFixed(2)}&nbsp;mm round: UK&nbsp;{us7.uk}, EU&nbsp;{us7.eu},
        Japan&nbsp;{us7.jp}. Going back, subtract {us0Mm.toFixed(4)} from the diameter and divide by
        {' '}{US_STEP_MM.toFixed(4)}: 18&nbsp;mm gives {dia18RawUs.toFixed(2)}, so US&nbsp;{dia18.us}.
      </p>

      <h3 id="uk" class="mt-10 text-xl font-semibold">UK, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand</h3>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-[62ch] text-text-muted">
        Letters, measured round the inside: A is {UK_BASE_CIRC_MM}&nbsp;mm and every letter adds
        {' '}{UK_STEP_MM}&nbsp;mm, with half letters between. After Z the scale carries on as Z+1,
        Z+2 and so on.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-[62ch] text-text-muted">
        To turn a letter into millimetres, count its steps from A and multiply by {UK_STEP_MM}. T is
        {' '}{ukStepsT} steps, so {UK_BASE_CIRC_MM} + {ukStepsT}&nbsp;×&nbsp;{UK_STEP_MM} ={' '}
        <strong class="text-text tabular">{ukT.circumferenceMm.toFixed(2)}&nbsp;mm</strong> round and
        {' '}{ukT.diameterMm.toFixed(2)}&nbsp;mm across: US&nbsp;{ukT.us}, EU&nbsp;{ukT.eu},
        Japan&nbsp;{ukT.jp}. M, the letter asked about most, is {ukM.circumferenceMm.toFixed(2)}&nbsp;mm
        round, which is US&nbsp;{ukM.us}.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-[62ch] text-text-muted">
        Going from a US size to a letter often lands on a half letter, because a US quarter and a UK
        half letter are almost, but not quite, the same step. If your jeweller stocks whole letters
        only, go up — the{' '}
        <a href="/#disagree" class="text-accent-text underline underline-offset-2">worked US&nbsp;6 example</a>
        {' '}shows why.
      </p>

      <h3 id="eu" class="mt-10 text-xl font-semibold">Europe (ISO 8653) and France</h3>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-[62ch] text-text-muted">
        The plainest system there is: the size is the inside circumference in millimetres, so a
        {' '}{eu54.eu} is {eu54.eu}&nbsp;mm round. ISO&nbsp;8653 defines sizes {ISO_MIN} to {ISO_MAX},
        and France uses the same numbers for its <em>taille</em>.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-[62ch] text-text-muted">
        Because a size is a whole millimetre, each one covers half a millimetre either side — which
        is why the chart above prints EU&nbsp;{eu54.eu} against both {eu54Rows.join(' and ')}. A ring
        made to exactly {eu54.eu}&nbsp;mm is US&nbsp;{eu54.us}. To convert, find your size in the{' '}
        <a href="#by-circumference" class="text-accent-text underline underline-offset-2">circumference table</a>:
        the row for {eu54.eu}&nbsp;mm is the row for EU&nbsp;{eu54.eu}.
      </p>

      <h3 id="japan" class="mt-10 text-xl font-semibold">Japan</h3>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-[62ch] text-text-muted">
        Japanese shops number rings 1 to {JP_MAX} (号, <em>gō</em>). Size&nbsp;1 is 13&nbsp;mm across
        and each size adds a third of a millimetre, so 15&nbsp;mm is a {jpAt(15)}, 16&nbsp;mm a
        {' '}{jpAt(16)} and 19&nbsp;mm a {jpAt(19)}. It is a trade convention: Japan&rsquo;s industrial
        standard, JIS&nbsp;S&nbsp;4700, numbers rings by circumference, like ISO.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-[62ch] text-text-muted">
        One Japanese step is {(1 / 3).toFixed(2)}&nbsp;mm of diameter against {usQuarterMm.toFixed(2)}&nbsp;mm
        for a US quarter, so the US column below moves unevenly — a quarter here, a half there. That
        is the two scales, not a gap. Sizes {jpFirst.jp} to {jpLast.jp} fall inside the chart&rsquo;s
        US&nbsp;{rows[0].us}–{rows[rows.length - 1].us} range:
      </p>

      <div class="table-scroll mt-5">
        <table class="w-full min-w-[34rem] text-left text-sm">
          <caption class="sr-only">
            Japanese ring sizes {jpFirst.jp} to {jpLast.jp} with inner diameter and circumference in
            millimetres and the nearest US, UK and EU size.
          </caption>
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-surface-sunk">
              <th scope="col" class="rounded-l-lg px-3 py-2 font-medium">Japan</th>
              <th scope="col" class="px-3 py-2 font-medium">Diameter</th>
              <th scope="col" class="px-3 py-2 font-medium">Circumference</th>
              <th scope="col" class="px-3 py-2 font-medium">US / CA</th>
              <th scope="col" class="px-3 py-2 font-medium">UK / AU</th>
              <th scope="col" class="rounded-r-lg px-3 py-2 font-medium">EU / ISO / FR</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="tabular">
            {JP_LOOKUP_ROWS.map((r) => (
              <tr class="border-t border-border">
                <th scope="row" class="px-3 py-2.5 font-medium text-text">{r.jp}</th>
                <td class="px-3 py-2.5">{r.diameterMm.toFixed(2)} mm</td>
                <td class="px-3 py-2.5">{r.circumferenceMm.toFixed(1)} mm</td>
                <td class="px-3 py-2.5">{r.us}</td>
                <td class="px-3 py-2.5">{r.uk ?? '—'}</td>
                <td class="px-3 py-2.5">{r.eu ?? '—'}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h3 id="india" class="mt-10 text-xl font-semibold">India</h3>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-[62ch] text-text-muted">
        India has no official ring size standard. Most Indian jewellers number rings by the inside
        circumference in millimetres minus 40, so an Indian {in14.in} is {in14.circumferenceMm.toFixed(0)}&nbsp;mm
        round — US&nbsp;{in14.us}, EU&nbsp;{in14.eu} — and that is what this site shows. Tanishq&rsquo;s
        chart runs about one size smaller. To convert, add 40 and read the{' '}
        <a href="#by-circumference" class="text-accent-text underline underline-offset-2">circumference table</a>;
        to be safe, give an Indian jeweller the diameter in millimetres, the one figure no chart can
        disagree about.{' '}
        <a href="/#disagree" class="text-accent-text underline underline-offset-2">How we checked</a>.
      </p>

      <h3 id="it-es-ch" class="mt-10 text-xl font-semibold">Italy, Spain, Switzerland and Brazil</h3>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-[62ch] text-text-muted">
        The same arithmetic as India — circumference minus 40 — and in Brazil a standard stands
        behind it: ABNT NBR 16058, aligned to ISO&nbsp;8653. A Brazilian aro {aro19.br} is
        {' '}{aro19.circumferenceMm.toFixed(0)}&nbsp;mm round and {aro19.diameterMm.toFixed(2)}&nbsp;mm
        across: US&nbsp;{aro19.us}, EU&nbsp;{aro19.eu}. An Italian {in14.it} is an Indian {in14.in} and
        a French {in14.fr}.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-[62ch] text-text-muted">
        Spain is the one to check before you buy. Some Spanish shops use the minus-40
        <em>talla</em> shown here; others label a talla as the plain ISO circumference, so their
        talla {eu54.eu} is {eu54.eu}&nbsp;mm round. Ask which scale a Spanish chart uses, or give the
        shop the millimetres.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- ── Sources ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
         The claim the whole site rests on. Short here; the full working is on
         the homepage — do not restate it. -->
    <section class="mt-16" aria-labelledby="sources">
      <h2 id="sources" class="text-[clamp(1.75rem,3.5vw,2.25rem)]">Where these numbers come from</h2>

      <div class="table-scroll mt-6">
        <table class="w-full min-w-[34rem] text-left text-sm">
          <caption class="sr-only">The standard behind each sizing system</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-surface-sunk">
              <th scope="col" class="rounded-l-lg px-3 py-2 font-medium">System</th>
              <th scope="col" class="px-3 py-2 font-medium">Standard</th>
              <th scope="col" class="rounded-r-lg px-3 py-2 font-medium">Rule</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr class="border-t border-border">
              <th scope="row" class="px-3 py-3 font-medium">EU / ISO / FR</th>
              <td class="px-3 py-3 text-text-muted">ISO 8653:2016</td>
              <td class="px-3 py-3 text-text-muted">
                Size = inner circumference in mm. France uses it too — taille 52 is 52 mm
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-t border-border">
              <th scope="row" class="px-3 py-3 font-medium">UK / AU</th>
              <td class="px-3 py-3 text-text-muted">BS 6820:1987 (attributed)</td>
              <td class="px-3 py-3 text-text-muted">C = 40 mm, +1.25 mm per letter</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-t border-border">
              <th scope="row" class="px-3 py-3 font-medium">Japan</th>
              {/* Was "JIS S 4700:2022", and that standard does not define this
                  scale: JIS S 4700 numbers rings by inner circumference in mm,
                  41 to 76 — the ISO number. The 号 scale Japanese shops label
                  rings with is the JCS trade convention (QA audit #6). */}
              <td class="px-3 py-3 text-text-muted">JCS trade scale (号). JIS S 4700 itself uses the ISO number</td>
              <td class="px-3 py-3 text-text-muted">Size 1 = 13 mm diameter, +⅓ mm per size</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-t border-border">
              <th scope="row" class="px-3 py-3 font-medium">Brazil</th>
              <td class="px-3 py-3 text-text-muted">ABNT NBR 16058</td>
              <td class="px-3 py-3 text-text-muted">Size = circumference − 40 (aligned to ISO 8653)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-t border-border">
              <th scope="row" class="px-3 py-3 font-medium">IT / ES / CH</th>
              <td class="px-3 py-3 text-text-muted">—</td>
              <td class="px-3 py-3 text-text-muted">Size = circumference − 40</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-t border-border">
              <th scope="row" class="px-3 py-3 font-medium">US / CA</th>
              <td class="px-3 py-3 text-text-muted">No governing standard</td>
              <td class="px-3 py-3 text-text-muted">Size 0 = 0.458 in, +0.032 in per size</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-t border-border">
              <th scope="row" class="px-3 py-3 font-medium">India</th>
              <td class="px-3 py-3 text-danger">No standard exists</td>
              <td class="px-3 py-3 text-text-muted">
                Circumference − 40, per four jewellers’ published charts
              </td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="mt-6 max-w-[62ch] text-text-muted">
        Two systems have no standard behind them, and we say so rather than presenting a number as
        settled. India has no BIS specification; Tanishq’s own chart runs about a size smaller than
        the convention four other Indian jewellers publish. UK letter charts vary by up to half a
        size depending on which half letters a given scale includes.
      </p>

      <p class="mt-4 max-w-[62ch] text-text-muted">
        The full working — including where four ranking sites give three different UK answers for
        the same ring — is on the{' '}
        <a href="/#disagree" class="text-accent-text underline underline-offset-2">ring sizer page</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <aside class="mt-16 rounded-xl border border-border bg-surface-sunk p-6">
      <p class="max-w-[62ch] text-text-muted">
        <strong class="text-text">A chart is only as good as the measurement you bring to it.</strong> If you are reading off a ring you own, measure the inside diameter carefully — a
        0.4&nbsp;mm error is a whole half size.{' '}
        <a href="/" class="text-accent-text underline underline-offset-2">Measure it against your screen</a>
        {' '}instead and the reading is taken for you.
      </p>
    </aside>

